<Base title="Import YouTube transcripts and podcasts | Lector" description="Two new ways to bring native audio and video into Lector: import a YouTube video's transcript with clickable timestamps, or upload a podcast and read along as it plays.">

<header class="docs-header">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">lector</a> / <a href="/blog/">blog</a> / youtube &amp; podcast import</p>
    <h1>Import YouTube transcripts and podcasts</h1>
    <p class="post-meta">15 July 2026 &middot; 4 min read &middot; Feature</p>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="container">
  <div class="docs-layout">
    <main class="docs-body">

      <p>The best input is the stuff you'd watch or listen to anyway. Two new
        import types bring it into Lector, where every word carries your
        known/unknown state, the translation drawer, and vocabulary mining:
        <strong>YouTube transcripts</strong> and <strong>podcast audio</strong>.
        Both live under the same <strong>Import</strong> menu.</p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/images/import-dropdown.png" alt="Lector's Import dropdown showing Import File, Import Audio, Import from URL, YouTube Transcript, and Paste Text.">
        <figcaption>Two new entries: <strong>Import Audio</strong> and <strong>YouTube Transcript</strong>.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="youtube">YouTube transcripts</h2>
      <p>Paste a video URL and pick a caption track — creator captions and
        auto-generated captions are labelled separately, so you know what you're
        getting. Lector imports the transcript as a normal lesson.</p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/images/youtube-caption-picker.png" alt="Lector's YouTube import dialog listing available caption tracks: English (creator), German (creator), and German (auto-generated).">
        <figcaption>Choose a language and caption source.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Every line keeps its timestamp. Click one and the embedded player jumps
        to that moment, so you can hear the sentence you're reading. The video is
        never downloaded or hosted — Lector reads the public transcript and links
        back to YouTube for playback.</p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/images/youtube-transcript-reader.png" alt="A YouTube transcript open in Lector: the embedded player at the top, then timestamped lines of German words, each clickable.">
        <figcaption>Read at your own pace; click a timestamp to jump the player there.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="podcasts">Podcast audio</h2>
      <p>Choose <strong>Import Audio</strong> and upload an episode (or any audio
        file). Lector transcribes it in the background with a Whisper model —
        local, or a hosted transcription backend if you've configured one — and
        turns it into a lesson.</p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/images/podcast-audio-import.png" alt="A library card in Lector for an imported audio file, labelled 'Audio import'.">
        <figcaption>The upload becomes a normal lesson once transcription finishes.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Then read along: press play and the current sentence highlights as the
        audio moves through it. <strong>Continuous</strong> plays straight
        through; <strong>Shadow</strong> is for shadowing practice. There's a
        speed control and sentence skip, and — as everywhere in Lector — every
        word is tappable for a translation or to save to your vocabulary.</p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/images/podcast-listen-along.png" alt="Lector's listen-along reader: Afrikaans transcript with the currently-playing sentence highlighted, and an audio player bar with a Continuous/Shadow toggle, speed control, and scrubber.">
        <figcaption>Listen-along: the sentence being spoken stays highlighted as you read.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="notes">Notes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>YouTube: not every video has captions; auto-generated ones can
          contain recognition errors (you can edit the transcript after import).
          The video is never downloaded or hosted.</li>
        <li>Podcasts: transcription accuracy depends on the audio and the model;
          the transcript is editable, and the audio stays on your own instance.</li>
        <li>A YouTube transcript is still useful even if the embedded player
          isn't available — the text stands on its own.</li>
        <li>Mining a sentence from a video carries a link back to its timestamp
          into your Anki export.</li>
      </ul>

